WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:08.840
This dodecahedral cage is made of silica
and it's tiny, really tiny.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.980
It was created by researchers in the States

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.760
who hope that one day it could
help in the fight against cancer.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.180
To give you a sense of just how
small this nanocage is

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.800
let's start with a single grain of salt,
it's around 300 microns wide.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.700
Compare that to say a red
blood cell, that's it there.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.040
Now compare the red blood
cell to something much

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.220
smaller like this the HIV virus.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.400
The silica nanocage is a bit more 
than ten times smaller than that.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.820
To make it researchers use 
a relatively simple technique.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.460
First they need a template. For
that they use soap molecules.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.680
When in solution the soap molecules
form tiny balls called micelles.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.115
The size of the micelles is precisely
controlled by the researchers.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.100
Then they add a silica precursor to the mix.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.920
Gradually the negatively charged
silica precursors are

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.728
attracted to the positively
charged micelle template.

00:01:22.728 --> 00:01:26.360
Over time they build up forming the structure.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.360
The template could
then be washed or burned out

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.300
which would leave a 
perfect dodecahedral cage.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.640
What's more this process happens without any intervention,

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.460
it's self assembling.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.000
This is how the researchers
think that the cages form

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.460
but until recently they
didn't even know they existed.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.580
They're so small it's nearly impossible
to snap a picture of them.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:55.940
To get that glimpse of the nanocages
researchers used cryo-electron microscopy.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:00.680
They put their sample on a tiny disc
and plunge it into liquid nitrogen

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.000
flash freezing it.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.420
Then they can use an electron
microscope to take a peak,

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.340
making images that look a bit like this.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.400
They repeated the process over and over

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.660
to gather nearly 20,000 images of
the fuzzy looking cages,

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:23.320
all lying in slightly different positions at
different angles and orientations.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.680
Now here's the clever bit. A computer
can analyze and average out all the

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.720
different images of all the different
orientations of the structure.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.120
So feed them into a computer and an algorithm
can reconstruct a 3D representation.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.600
And what popped out was a perfect
dodecahedron, only 11 nanometers across.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.440
Studies have shown that ultra-small
silica particles can easily and safely

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.860
pass through the body being excreted in
the urine, rather than getting stuck in

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.480
the liver which can cause side effects.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.100
Perhaps nanocages like these
could be used as containers

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.880
for things like
anti-cancer drugs

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.640
or as ultra small vehicles to carry diagnostic molecules.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.100
But that is still a long way off.

